<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    package="com.rkskmp">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
        F-14 hardening: legacy shared-storage permissions are scoped to the API
        range where they actually grant something. Publishing them unbounded
        surfaces a scary "modify or delete your storage" grant in the OS
        permission list on modern devices, and gets flagged by the Play
        Console pre-launch report as an unjustified sensitive permission
        even though on API 30+ the OS silently ignores them.

        Attack surface being closed:
          * API 19-28: WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE grants write access to the
            ENTIRE `/sdcard/*` (every other app's shared files, DCIM,
            Downloads, Pictures, etc.) — enormously broader than the app's
            single "save downloaded PDF to Downloads" use case.
          * API 29+ : WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is a no-op for user files but
            still appears on the permission grant screen, degrading trust.
          * API 33+ : READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is superseded by the per-media
            READ_MEDIA_* permissions and has no effect.

        Why `tools:replace="android:maxSdkVersion"` is REQUIRED:
        Two transitive React Native libraries declare these permissions in
        their own AndroidManifest.xml WITHOUT a maxSdkVersion:
            node_modules/react-native-blob-util/android/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml
                <uses-permission WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE />
                <uses-permission READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE />
            node_modules/react-native-fs/android/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml
                <uses-permission WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE />
        Without the marker, AGP's manifest merger keeps the library
        declaration (un-bounded) and drops our maxSdkVersion, silently
        defeating the fix. `tools:replace` forces our attribute to win.
        See https://developer.android.com/build/manage-manifests#markers

        Functional impact:
          * downloadFile() in src/utils/helper.js uses ReactNativeBlobUtil
            with `useDownloadManager: true`, i.e. it delegates writes to
            Android's system DownloadManager service. From API 29+ that
            service places files in public Downloads WITHOUT requiring
            the app to hold WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE.
          * All app-internal file I/O (RNFS.stat on picker output,
            image / video compression cache) targets app-private paths
            (getExternalFilesDir / cacheDir), which never require these
            permissions on API 19+.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="28"
        tools:replace="android:maxSdkVersion" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32"
        tools:replace="android:maxSdkVersion" />

    <!--
        F-14 hardening: modern per-media read permissions (API 33+).

        These MUST be declared because src/route/index.js requests them at
        runtime for API 33+ devices:

            Platform.Version >= 33
              ? [ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION, ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION,
                 READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE,       // scoped to <=32 by manifest
                 READ_MEDIA_IMAGES,           // declared below
                 READ_MEDIA_VIDEO,            // declared below
                 CAMERA]

        Prior to F-14 both READ_MEDIA_* were commented out of the manifest,
        so on Android 13+ devices the runtime `PermissionsAndroid.request(
        READ_MEDIA_IMAGES)` call was silently auto-denied by the OS (an
        app cannot request a runtime permission it did not declare) and
        image-picker gallery reads relied on the fallback ACTION_GET_CONTENT
        path.

        READ_MEDIA_AUDIO is intentionally NOT declared — the app has no
        audio-file read use case, and adding it would enlarge the grant
        surface without justification.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_IMAGES" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_VIDEO" />

    <!-- For background services permission -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <application
      android:name=".MainApplication"
      android:label="@string/app_name"
      android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
      android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
      android:allowBackup="false"
      android:theme="@style/AppTheme"
      android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"
      android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
      android:supportsRtl="true">

      <!--
          F-19 hardening: strip the vestigial org.apache.http.legacy declaration.

          `com.google.android.gms:play-services-maps:19.1.0` (transitively pulled
          in by react-native-maps) injects the following into its own AAR's
          AndroidManifest.xml at line 33:

              <uses-library android:name="org.apache.http.legacy"
                            android:required="false" />

          The manifest merger then propagates it into the app's final manifest
          under <application>. That declaration exists purely as a compat
          safety-net for consumers on very old Play Services versions that
          still had code paths reaching `org.apache.http.HttpClient`; Maps SDK
          runtime has been on OkHttp / HttpURLConnection since v18+ and does
          not actually reach into the legacy library.

          Leaving it in has real cost:
             1. `org.apache.http.legacy.jar` shipped by AOSP is Apache
                HttpClient 4.x code frozen circa 2013–2015 and has multiple
                unpatched CVEs (CVE-2014-3577 hostname bypass, CVE-2015-5262
                TLS MitM, CVE-2020-13956 URI parsing) that will never be
                back-ported because the artefact is unmaintained.
             2. It signals to security scanners (MobSF, AppSweep, Play Console
                pre-launch) that legacy HTTP is available to the app.
             3. It expands the trusted classpath — any dependency, or an
                attacker who lands reflective code, can invoke
                `org.apache.http.*` classes with weak TLS defaults.

          RKSK's own Java/Kotlin surface and every react-native module we ship
          are verified free of `org.apache.http.*` imports (see
          android/APACHE_HTTP_LEGACY.md §Verification). We therefore instruct
          the manifest merger to REMOVE the play-services-maps-injected node
          via `tools:node="remove"`. The `android:name` value must match the
          library's declaration verbatim — the merger identifies uses-library
          nodes by their `android:name` and the removal only fires on an
          exact match.

          If a future dependency legitimately needs Apache HTTP, we must first
          replace it with a maintained client (OkHttp, ktor, Retrofit),
          not restore this node.
      -->
      <uses-library
          android:name="org.apache.http.legacy"
          android:required="false"
          tools:node="remove" />

      <!-- Add this service for background actions -->
        <service 
          android:name="com.asterinet.react.bgactions.RNBackgroundActionsTask"
          android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync"
        />

        <!--
            F-06 hardening: react-native-webview ships a FileProvider whose default
            @xml/file_provider_paths maps `<external-path path="."/>` — i.e. the
            entire shared external storage root. We override the meta-data via
            manifest merger (`tools:replace="android:resource"`) so this provider
            binds to our narrow @xml/rksk_webview_file_paths, which only exposes
            the app-private external files directory that RNCWebView actually
            writes captured media into.

            The authority (`${applicationId}.fileprovider`) is kept because the
            library's runtime code hardcodes `getPackageName() + ".fileprovider"`
            in RNCWebViewModuleImpl.getOutputUri(). The duplicate-authority half
            of F-06 is resolved by removing react-native-send-intent from the
            dependency tree, so no other library now competes for this authority.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="com.reactnativecommunity.webview.RNCWebViewFileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true"
            tools:replace="android:authorities,android:exported,android:grantUriPermissions">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/rksk_webview_file_paths"
                tools:replace="android:resource" />
        </provider>

        <!--
            F-08 hardening: the Google Maps SDK for Android reads its API key
            from this meta-data at runtime, so the string MUST end up in the
            packaged APK. What we control is that the string never lives in
            source control.

            The value is injected at build time via manifestPlaceholders
            configured in android/app/build.gradle. Developers set
            GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY in ~/.gradle/gradle.properties (per-user,
            outside VCS); CI supplies it via ORG_GRADLE_PROJECT_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY.
            See android/SECRETS.md for setup and for the Cloud Console
            restrictions that keep the key useless if it is extracted from
            a released APK.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.geo.API_KEY"
            android:value="${GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY}" />
      <!--
          F-11 hardening: MainActivity previously carried a second intent-filter
          declaring ACTION_VIEW + CATEGORY_DEFAULT + CATEGORY_BROWSABLE with no
          <data> element. Because no scheme / host / mimeType was pinned, the
          filter matched *any* Intent.ACTION_VIEW dispatched without a URI —
          i.e. any app on the device could invoke:

              Intent i = new Intent(Intent.ACTION_VIEW);
              startActivity(i);

          and RKSK's MainActivity would appear as a resolution candidate
          (or be launched outright when no other handler matched). Combined
          with android:launchMode="singleTask", this widened the surface for:

            - task-hijack / task-affinity attacks (StrandHogg-style),
            - unwanted activity reordering during sensitive flows
              (JWT-carrying requests, camera capture, PDF viewing),
            - forced foregrounding by malicious co-installed apps.

          The filter was also broken as a *deep-link* handler because deep
          links require a `<data>` element (scheme + host + optional
          pathPrefix / pathPattern) to route from an https:// or custom-scheme
          URI to the app.

          Audit of the app confirmed the filter was dead code:
            - `MainActivity.kt` does not override onNewIntent() and never
              reads intent.getData().
            - The JS side has zero `Linking.getInitialURL()` /
              `Linking.addEventListener('url', …)` handlers (only outbound
              Linking.openURL() calls: settings deep-links, Play Store).
            - No Firebase Dynamic Links, no OAuth redirect callbacks, no
              custom URL scheme, no react-navigation `linking` config.

          The safe fix is therefore to remove the malformed filter entirely.
          The MAIN + LAUNCHER filter below is retained (it's how the OS
          launches the app icon), and android:exported="true" is REQUIRED
          for that — do NOT flip it to "false". `exported="true"` is safe
          precisely because MAIN + LAUNCHER is the only remaining filter.

          If in the FUTURE the app genuinely needs to consume a deep link
          (OTP magic link, share intent, OAuth callback, etc.), add a
          *separate* intent-filter with a fully-specified <data> block
          scoped to the exact scheme + host you own, e.g.:

              <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
                  <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                  <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                  <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                  <data android:scheme="https"
                        android:host="rksk.nhmmp.gov.in"
                        android:pathPrefix="/app/" />
              </intent-filter>

          …plus host a Digital Asset Links `assetlinks.json` file on that
          domain so the OS verifies ownership (`autoVerify="true"`), and
          add matching JS-side Linking handlers to actually consume the URL
          instead of the current no-op. Never re-introduce a VIEW filter
          without a `<data>` element.
      -->
      <activity
        android:name=".MainActivity"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:configChanges="keyboard|keyboardHidden|orientation|screenLayout|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|uiMode"
        android:launchMode="singleTask"
        android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
        android:screenOrientation="portrait"
        android:exported="true">
        <intent-filter>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent-filter>
      </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
